#!/usr/bin/env bash
# post-merge — post the 📝 product report for any `main` commit that has not had one yet.
#
# The deterministic local trigger for the prose rail. `git pull` / `git merge` on main is the exact
# moment new shipped commits arrive in this checkout, so it is when a report is owed. No schedule to
# interpret, no cloud runner, no model deciding when to fire.
#
# Why a hook and not CI: the prose writers (`devin`, `agy`) authenticate through interactive OAuth and
# expose no headless credential path, so a GitHub Actions runner cannot produce prose at all
# (Roadmap/LEARNINGS.md). The mechanical 📦/🚀 pings stay in the workflow where they belong; the prose
# is local by necessity, and this makes it automatic rather than remembered.
#
# ── Three properties this hook must have, in order of importance ───────────────────────────────
# 1. NEVER block or fail a git operation. A prose model being slow or down must not make `git pull`
#    look broken. Every path exits 0.
# 2. NEVER post twice for the same commit — report-new-commits.mjs owns that via its state file.
# 3. Be silent when there is nothing to do, so a normal pull looks normal.

# ── Why this ALSO runs from post-checkout (added 2026-07-27) ───────────────────────────────────
# The rail stalled for a full day and the cause was not the hook or the writers, it was the RETRY
# TRIGGER. On 2026-07-26 both prose writers were transiently unavailable at the same moment, the
# reporter correctly left its baseline where it was and logged "it will be retried on the next run"
# — and then no next run came. Four shipped commits went unreported.
#
# `post-merge` fires on `git pull`/`git merge`. But main moves here two ways that never produce a
# local merge: a squash merge performed SERVER-side via `gh pr merge`, and a docs commit pushed
# straight to main. So the retry depended on an event that had largely stopped happening.
#
# **A retry whose trigger may never recur is not a retry.** The same script is therefore invoked from
# `post-checkout` as well: `git checkout main` is the other reliable "I am now on main and it may
# have moved" moment, and it is exactly what follows a `gh pr merge`. Both are safe to over-trigger —
# report-new-commits.mjs owns the once-per-commit guarantee via its state file, prints nothing when
# there is nothing to do, and cannot post twice.

# Only when `main` is the branch that just moved. The hook also fires on feature-branch merges, and a
# feature commit is not something to report.
branch="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo '')"
[ "$branch" = "main" ] || exit 0

# GB_SKIP_COMMIT_REPORT=1 for a bulk pull or a rebase where reports would be noise.
[ -n "$GB_SKIP_COMMIT_REPORT" ] && exit 0

command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0

root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || echo '')"
[ -n "$root" ] || exit 0
[ -f "$root/scripts/report-new-commits.mjs" ] || exit 0

# Backgrounded and fully detached: a prose model can take a minute, and property 1 says `git pull`
# must not wait for it. Output goes to a log rather than the terminal so a pull stays readable, and
# `|| true` means even a crash in the reporter cannot affect git's exit status.
(
  node "$root/scripts/report-new-commits.mjs" >>"$root/.git/gb-commit-report.log" 2>&1 || true
) &

exit 0
